# Getting Started Tutorial — Jetson Orin Nano

This tutorial explains the steps needed to connect to the Jetson Orin Nano from a PC, activate headless VNC screen sharing (without a physical monitor connected), and then run the Python detection script using a `.sh` script.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

- Windows PC
- A flashed and configured Jetson Orin Nano
- A USB cable for the PC ↔ Jetson link (make sure the cable supports data transfer)
- [TightVNC](https://www.tightvnc.com/) installed on the PC (open source VNC client)
- A working SSH access

---

## 2. Connecting to the Jetson via SSH (USB link)

The official NVIDIA method consists of controlling the Jetson over SSH via a USB link, before installing a VNC server to access the graphical desktop.

1. Connect the Jetson to the PC using the USB cable (connect it to the USB-C port on the Jetson).
2. From a terminal, connect via SSH to the Jetson's default address:

```bash
ssh fire-alarm-ia@192.168.55.1
```

> **Jetson IP address:** `192.168.55.1`
> This same address will be used later in the dashboard (`ws://192.168.55.1:8765`).

---

## 3. Activating headless VNC screen sharing (this part is already working)

The official NVIDIA method assumes a physical monitor is connected to the Jetson to activate the VNC server. In our case, however, no monitor is permanently available (the HDMI-DisplayPort adapter is shared between several groups). VNC therefore needs to be activated **without a wired display connection ("headless")**.

**WARNING**: This EDID file is already set up. It is documented here in case you ever need to rework this part.

### 3.1 Using an EDID file

To simulate the presence of a monitor, an EDID file matching a virtual monitor is used:

```
EDID file: AOC0000 monitor, 1920x1080 resolution
```

This file makes the Jetson believe a monitor is physically connected, which allows the graphical server and VNC to start without a wired connection.

The full procedure (retrieving and installing the EDID file, configuring the graphics driver) is detailed in this community repository:
👉 [jetson-headless-vnc (GitHub)](https://github.com/mauroarcidiacono/jetson-headless-vnc)

Follow the tutorial step by step.

### 3.2 Starting the VNC server on the Jetson

Once the EDID file is in place, start the `x11vnc` server on the Jetson (over SSH):

```bash
x11vnc -usepw -forever -display :0
```

- `-usepw`: requires a password on connection
- `-forever`: keeps the server running after the first client disconnects
- `-display :0`: targets the main display simulated by the EDID

### 3.3 Connecting with TightVNC from the PC

1. Open TightVNC Viewer on your PC.
2. Enter the Jetson's IP address: `192.168.55.1`
3. Enter the password set on the server side.
4. The Jetson's graphical desktop is then displayed on your screen, without an HDMI/DisplayPort cable.

