## 4. Running the AI via the alias

The Jetson and the camera work together using a Python script that manages the video stream coming from the camera and the AI. This Python script is already set up on the Jetson, but you can find it in this repo [here](/home/andy/repo/IOC-DUT/Fire-Detection-IA/Script-IA/fire-detect.py). We also used another Python script to send our data to the dashboard; this script can be found [here](/home/andy/repo/IOC-DUT/Fire-Detection-IA/Script-IA/serveur_relais.py). Both of them need to be running at the same time. These files can be found on the Jetson Nano in `/home/Documents/Fire-Detect`.

To avoid running them manually, we wrote a script, `~/run.sh`, to run both files and get the project running.
Also, to avoid typing `cd ~/Documents/Fire-Detect && ./run.sh` every time, we created an alias in the `~/.bashrc` file. The alias is `fire-detect`.

So, to run the project, you just have to type in a terminal:

```running
fire-detect
```

Do not forget to connect the camera to the Ethernet port of the Jetson. If the camera is not working, verify that the Ethernet port is configured as follows:

```Ethernet
IP address : 192.168.0.100
IP mask : 255.255.255.0
```

---

## 5. Launching the dashboard (on the PC side)

The React dashboard used to visualize alerts in real time is launched from the PC (currently Windows only, as the command does not work on Linux).

Navigate to the project folder:

```
/home/andy/repo/IOC-DUT/Vietnamese-Code/New-Fire-Alarm/fire-dashboard
```

Then, in a Windows terminal:

```bash
# Only needed on first launch
npm install

# Launch the dashboard
npm run dev
```

In the dashboard interface, enter the Jetson's WebSocket address:

```
ws://192.168.55.1:8765
```

---

## 6. Full workflow summary

1. Connect the Jetson via USB → SSH connection (`192.168.55.1`)
2. Activate headless VNC using the EDID file + `x11vnc`
3. Connect with TightVNC to access the graphical desktop
4. Launch detection with the `fire-detect` command (`.sh` script)
5. Launch the dashboard on the PC side with `npm run dev`
6. Connect the dashboard to the Jetson via `ws://192.168.55.1:8765`